;; Verify that auto-drop defers do not create false elision barriers.
;;
;; When `x` is bound to (rc/of ...), an auto-drop defer is injected at
;; the end of the let body: (defer (rc/drop x)).
;;
;; When `c` is bound to (rc/clone x) in the same let, and the user
;; explicitly drops `c` via (rc/drop c), the auto-drop for `c` is
;; suppressed (consumption detection) and the clone/drop pair is an
;; elision candidate.
;;
;; The elision is eligible because:
;;   - (rc/drop c) is the first item in the do-block (use_idx = 0)
;;   - the auto-drop defer for `x` is appended AFTER all user code
;;   - no barrier appears before use_idx = 0
;;
;; Elision barrier semantics: auto-drop defers are always appended at
;; the end of the let body do-block. They appear AFTER any explicit
;; clone/drop pairs written by the user. Therefore they never act as
;; barriers for elision candidates that appear earlier in the do-block.
;;
;; Expected codegen: no rc_strong_increment / rc_strong_decrement for c.
;; The strong count of x stays 1 throughout (the clone pair is skipped).
(defn main [] :int
  (let [x (rc/of 42)
        c (rc/clone x)]
    (rc/drop c)
    (println (rc/strong-count x))
    0))
